sourceReview.sourceSolution:
The velocity is the gradient of the displacement graph. The displacement has stationary points at $t=\frac{4}{9}$ and $t=2$, so $v(t)=0$ at those values. The displacement is decreasing, then increasing, then decreasing, so the velocity is negative, then positive, then negative.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.2,0) -- (4.3,0);
\draw[->] (0,-1.8) -- (0,2.2);
\node[right] at (4.3,0) {$t$};
\node[above] at (0,2.2) {$v(t)$};
\draw[smooth] plot coordinates {(0,-1.2) (0.7,0) (1.8,1.25) (3.0,0) (3.8,-1.4)};
\fill (0.7,0) circle (0.05);
\fill (3.0,0) circle (0.05);
\node[below] at (0.7,0) {$\frac{4}{9}$};
\node[below] at (3.0,0) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

